= Integrated drive tests and Parameter A/B

Open *Drive tests* from project navigation, *Open drive tests* in Project Overview, a Task row's *Drive tests* action, or a saved route's *Open drive tests* action. The workspace follows Region / Project / Task / Route / Test ID hierarchy and uses the viewer's green palette, typography and common basemap library. Save or discard Task changes before leaving. *Back to planning* restores the region, project and saved Task. One route is selected per execution; separate routes remain separate runs.

The workspace combines Test setup, Replay, KPI analytics, Investigate and Test report. Imported, synthetic demonstration and simulated Test IDs have distinct source labels. Import normalized UTF-8 CSV with timestamp, latitude, longitude and RAT headers, plus optional RF/service fields. One device per file, explicit timestamp offset, unique timestamps within each RAT and paired or missing GPS are required. Limits are 16 MiB and 100,000 observations. Validation precedes persistence and the original file SHA-256 is retained; original source bytes must be archived separately.

Replay synchronizes the trace cursor, UTC sample inspector, timeline and event list. Offline trace view works without tiles; NDT street map uses the same OpenFreeMap style as the main viewer. Missing GPS and temporal gaps break traces. Threshold excursions and observed cell changes describe the data; they do not establish handover outcomes. Planning runs label strongest-transmitter changes as predicted selections.

Analysis profiles persist separate LTE/NR thresholds for six measured metrics and planning received power, along with evidence criteria. Revision checks reject stale saves and keep the analyst's draft available. Readiness is assessed separately per selected RAT and metric. CDFs and whole-capture percentiles remain descriptive, sample-weighted statistics. Investigate searches and paginates observations, links findings to their source sample, and retains issue lifecycle and profile history. JSON includes complete current observations, source hashes, the effective profile and saved route comparison when present. Reports add a frozen route/experiment provenance sheet. CSV exports normalized fields.

== Task routes and measured traces

Test setup loads the served planning database's actual saved routes and speed cases. A launch freezes route/network context with hashes; a changed context rejects the launch until *Refresh planning inputs*. New road fits preserve complete `originalCoordinates`. Previously fitted records can lack their original geometry. Routes remain provisional or legacy-fit-unverified because fitting does not independently establish intended-road accuracy.

An imported capture can be associated with its selected Task route in forward or reverse direction. The association stores chainage, lateral error and matched, missing-GPS, off-route, ambiguous or wrong-direction status separately from raw observations. The current policy uses a 50 m tolerance and 10 s continuity cutoff. Associations are versioned and report continuous route coverage. This is a conservative single-pass geometric projection, not a road-network map matcher; repeated laps require separate captures or review.

== Virtual drives and power A/B

Choose one saved route, one or more saved speed cases, an assumed RAT label, LOS/NLOS, EIRP and a 0.1–10 s sampling interval. The initial model creates one isotropic planning transmitter per eligible Bell site at its lowest reported usable frequency. It uses fixed base/UE heights of 25 m and 1.5 m and the existing UMa mean path-loss formula within 10 m to 5 km. Missing or out-of-domain results remain missing. `predicted_rx_dbm` is planning received power, never measured RSRP or SS-RSRP. Static Virtual UE positions are not moved.

*Virtual drive* generates one run per speed. *Parameter A/B* sets a common baseline EIRP A across the frozen transmitter set and changes the selected transmitter's EIRP in B. Every pair shares route, speed, time grid, model and evaluation profile. Cases 20, 40 and 60 km/h produce A20/B20, A40/B40 and A60/B60. This static model gives speed a trajectory/time role; it does not model an independent speed-dependent RF effect. Antenna patterns/tilt, interference, fading, mobility procedures, throughput and latency are unavailable. New York reservation data lacks the required radio inputs, so import, association and replay remain available there while simulation is disabled.

A bounded local worker keeps queued, running, completed, failed, cancelled and interrupted experiment state in SQLite. Limits are 20 pending jobs, 100,000 observations per run and 200,000 total per experiment. Complete member Test IDs are published transactionally. Cancellation or partial failure cannot produce a completed experiment; completed members remain inspectable. A server interruption preserves history and requires a new submission for the interrupted job.

== Route comparison and reproducibility

KPI analytics can compare a baseline A and current B on common 20 m route bins. Both captures need the same geometry and Task; imported captures also need compatible direction/associations and adequate continuous coverage. The comparison requires at least 80 percent common coverage for every represented RAT. Power averages use linear power before conversion to dB. The saved result shows paired coverage, B minus A, a difference map and a keyboard-accessible position selector linked to Replay. Whole-capture distributions keep their separate sample-weighted meaning.

Simulated A/B quantifies the declared model's parameter effect. Imported A/B remains observational because device, traffic and capture conditions may differ. Cross-origin calibration is unavailable in the initial model. A comparison freezes both observation sets, hashes, parameters, association policies and evaluation profile; later planning, association or profile edits cannot change it. *Export experiment JSON* retains the completed speed/variant matrix and its comparisons.

